        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Post-flight, astronauts are four times more likely to suffer a herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP or ‘slipped disk’) than the general population, with the greatest risk in the first 12 months after landing. This adversity may be related to the prolonged offloading of the spine and its surrounding structures during spaceflight, resulting in changes to intervertebral disk structure and physiology and a general straightening of the spine. The resulting condition of the spine may leave the spine vulnerable upon return to Earth and re-exposure to gravity. 'SkinSuit' is a specialized garment designed to provide an axial load to the spine and its surrounding structures, and could potentially contribute to maintaining spinal health during prolonged microgravity exposure and reduce the risk of post-flight injury with re-exposure to gravity.
<p>
The specific goal of this study is to perform a SkinSuit Operational Evaluation (OE) in microgravity to:<ol><br>
<li>Evaluate the efficacy of SkinSuit in modulating stature of the spine.
<li>Evaluate SkinSuit operational considerations such as comfort, thermoregulation, donning/doffing, impingement, and range of motion to prepare for long-duration missions.</ol>
<p>
The long-term objectives of this study relate to the development of a passive countermeasure to:<ol><br>
<li>Prevent spine elongation during flight to help counteract the post-flight risk of back injury and alleviate back pain felt by astronauts in flight.
<li>Increase understanding of the prevention of back pain and the reduction of risk of post-mission back injury in order to increase healthcare and space flight knowledge. </ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Preflight, body measurements will be taken to tailor the SkinSuit. A follow-up session to confirm or modify the suit fit will be completed. Baseline data will be collected to measure height, posture (standing, supine, hanging), and range of motion. Crewmembers will provide a subjective assessment of the ease of donning and doffing the suit as well as its comfort level.<p>
The crewmember will wear SkinSuit between 4 and 8 days for approximately 4-8 hours between the Morning and Evening Daily Planning Conferences (DPC) while on the International Space Station (ISS).  Immediately before donning and doffing on each SkinSuit day, height will be measured and a short questionnaire including comfort rating scales will be completed.  
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Results for this investigation are unavailable. This is an international experiment. NASA does not currently have an agreement with international space partners to archive their data in the LSDA.
<p>
